# gorb-os — agent notes

Independent ESP-IDF firmware project (see `README.md`). This file only records
the gotchas that cost real time on the bench; it does not restate the README.

## Long-running bench commands must run detached — the 30 s tool cap is real

**Rule: anything that takes over 30 s MUST be launched in the background and
polled, never run inline.** A command that runs past ~30 s is killed by the tool
timeout *mid-flight*, which for these operations is not just an annoyance — a
killed WiFi OTA leaves a half-written inactive slot, and you pay the wait twice
(kill, then re-issue). The reliable idiom:

```
# write the work into /tmp/foo.sh, then launch fully detached and return now:
setsid /tmp/foo.sh </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 & disown
# ...the script itself appends "EXIT=$?" / "DONE $(date +%T)" to a /tmp log,
# and you poll that log with short `sleep N; cat/grep <log>` calls.
```

Two launch gotchas that burned time this session, both avoided by using a
**script file** rather than an inline one-liner:
- `nohup curl … &` alone still blocks the tool call — you must `setsid … &
  disown` AND redirect stdin/stdout/stderr (`</dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1`) for it
  to actually detach and return immediately.
- Nested double-quotes inside an inline `bash -c '…'` launcher break the shell.
  Put the payload in a file instead of quoting it around.
- Each command in a `run_commands` array is its **own shell**; a `cd` in one
  element does not carry to the next. Prefix every element with its own `cd`,
  or use absolute paths.

### Known operations that exceed 30 s (run these detached)
- **WiFi OTA push**: `curl --data-binary @build/gorb-os.bin
  http://<ip>/api/v1/ota` — ~40 s for the ~1.3 MB app over a healthy link, and
  it **aborts mid-stream** ("upload aborted by client") when rssi is marginal
  (< ~-70, ICMP loss). Prefer **serial flash** for the app on a flaky bench
  (`/tmp/serflash.sh` does app+otadata to ota_0 in ~25 s, hashes verified); use
  WiFi OTA only when the device is near its AP. The device keeps booting the old
  slot during a push, so it looks "silent" — that is the httpd worker blocked in
  the flash write, not a hang; poll, don't re-push (a second push corrupts).
- **Serial flash of a bulk image**: any `esptool write_flash` / `idf.py flash`
  that sends the app (1.3 MB) — and especially anything that re-sends
  `assets/clock_assets.bin` (~1.85 MB) — runs 25–40 s+. Detach and poll
  `/tmp/serflash-<ORB_LABEL>.log` (per orb) for `Hash of data verified` + `EXIT=0`.
- **`idf.py build` that recompiles many objects**: the steady-state rebuild of
  one file (`orb_net.c`) is ~10 s and is fine inline, but the **first build into
  a fresh `-B` dir**, or any build after an `sdkconfig`/Kconfig change that
  drags in mbedtls/LWIP, exceeds 30 s. Run detached and grep the log for
  `Project build complete` / `binary size` / `warning:` / `error:`.
- **A chip wipe — `orb-manager` `POST /api/wipe`, an orb card's Wipe chip
  button, or `orbflash -mode wipe`.** The 16 MB ROM erase itself is tens of
  seconds, and the blank-check digest that follows costs about a minute more on
  the wire, so the whole operation is a couple of minutes with nothing printed
  during it. Run detached.
- **`idf.py flash monitor`**, and any `monitor`/serial capture held open longer
  than ~25 s (e.g. watching the association ladder). Use the short bounded
  capture scripts (`tools/capf.py <secs> <log> [--no-reset]`,
  `tools/cap.py <secs> [reset]`) with a duration under ~12 s inline; longer
  captures go detached.

## Wiping an orb: the ROM has no chip-erase opcode, and `FLASH_MD5` needs a preamble

Measured on two orbs, 2026-10-14, while implementing `esprom.Wipe`.

- **There is no ROM chip erase.** esptool marks both `erase_flash` (0xD0) and
  `erase_region` (0xD1) `@stub_function_only`, so a bare S3 ROM rejects them and
  this project uploads no stub. The ROM's genuine erase is `FLASH_BEGIN`, which
  erases its whole range before accepting any data: `num_blocks=0` writes nothing,
  so offset 0 with `erase_size` = capacity **is** the whole-chip erase. That is the
  same request `EraseRegion` already sends for the factory reset, one bigger number.
- **`FLASH_MD5` (0x13) is a ROM command, but only after `SPI_SET_PARAMS`.** Attach
  and hash without configuring the geometry and the ROM answers status `[1,9]`,
  which looks exactly like an unsupported opcode and is not. esptool's no-stub
  `verify_flash` prints "Enabling default SPI flash mode" then "Configuring flash
  size" for precisely this reason. The reply layout is unusual too: 32 hex chars
  FIRST, status bytes after them, so `checkCommand` cannot parse this one response.
- **An erase is proven, not believed.** An erased flash reads all `0xFF`, so the
  check is `md5(0xFF × capacity)`; for the orb's 16 MB that is
  `d5929b7a5ad99f511fc83d7d0b48b85f`. A wipe that cannot show that digest returns
  an error rather than a success.
- **A wiped orb is dark, not bricked.** `/api/ports` still lists the port (the host
  reads the USB descriptors, and the `44:BD:8D:…` serial number is the burned eFuse
  MAC) with `probed:false` and no identity. Nothing prints, no AP opens, nothing
  announces. The wipe also drops the hub's own memory of that board — fleet row,
  cached name, address grant — so **`/api/fleet` showing one board fewer is the
  expected result, not a lost row.** A board with nothing on it reports
  "no gorb-os firmware booted" and the `Upgrade needed` verdict. Flash it back.
- **A blank flash makes an S3 boot-loop, and the flapping port is the expected post-wipe
  state — not a fault.** Descriptors and the `44:BD:8D:…` eFuse serial come from ROM
  silicon, so a blank chip is still visible; but with no valid image the ROM bootloader
  reloads every boot, so the port comes and goes every few seconds. Measured on both
  wiped orbs (2026-10-14): enumerated as `303a:1001`, `cdc_acm` bound the ACM device,
  then `vhci_hcd: connection closed` about 1–3 s later, repeating forever. The symptom
  mimics a bad link exactly, so stop here before blaming cable, hub, rail or usbip.
- **Recover a blank board with the manual BOOT-to-download sequence.** Hold BOOT, tap
  and release RESET, keep holding BOOT a second, then release. That drops the ROM into
  the download mode instead of the boot loop, the port stops flapping, and the hub can
  talk to it. A **wipe should warn the operator to expect this**; a freshly wiped board
  is unreachable by software until someone presses the buttons.
- **Idle presence is not a health test, and neither is a DTR/RTS drop.** A boot-looping
  orb reads `present=True status=OK` on the host between reloads, and asserting DTR/RTS
  drives the auto-reset circuit so the handle dies inside ~2 s on a good board. Clear a
  board of hardware by forcing download mode and seeing it hold. `vhci_hcd: connection
  closed` only says the host tore the session down; it names neither device nor cause.

### Known operations that are SAFE inline (under 30 s)
- `python3 tools/host_tz_test.py` (~1 s), `python3 tools/host_sd_test.py` (~2 s),
  `python3 tools/host_mdns_test.py` (~4 s, and ~4 min with `--mutations`).
- Steady-state `idf.py build` after editing a single source file (~10 s).
- `esptool erase_region` / a status `curl` / `ping` / `nm`+`strings` on an elf.
- Short serial captures (≤ ~12 s) like `tools/capf.py 8 /tmp/x.txt --no-reset`.

## `version.txt` is tracked, `PROJECT_VER` is cached — a bump with no reconfigure stamps the old version

`tools/orb_version.sh` composes the stamp (`v1.0.0+92424ff-dirty`) and root
`CMakeLists.txt` runs it at **configure** time. `idf.py build` alone recompiles changed
sources and keeps the cached stamp, so an image carrying new code can print an old
version. The fix after any `version.txt` edit is `idf.py reconfigure && idf.py build`.
`tools/serflash.sh` reads the stamp back out of `build/gorb-os.bin` and refuses the
flash on a mismatch, so the failure is loud — but only at flash time. `idf.py
set-version` does not help: it re-runs IDF's own finder, which never sees `version.txt`
once `PROJECT_VER` is set. Run `tools/orb_version.sh --self-test` (~2 s, no toolchain)
after touching the composer; the hub's Go suite does not run it.

## sdkconfig is local + untracked — a NEW Kconfig `default y` does NOT appear on a rebuild

Only `sdkconfig` is untracked here (there is no `*_defconfig`; `sdkconfig.defaults`
IS tracked, and a new option needs a line in it for a fresh clone to get it).
`idf.py build` treats an existing `sdkconfig` as authoritative:
adding a `config FOO` with `default y` to `main/Kconfig` does **not** materialize
`FOO=y` into the existing `sdkconfig`/`build/config/sdkconfig.h` — so
`#if CONFIG_FOO` silently compiles the feature **out**, and the only tell is a
binary that barely grows. This bit `CONFIG_ORB_NET_CAPTIVE_DNS`: the first "clean
build" was a false pass (+96 B = just the URI strings, not the task).

Therefore, **whenever you add a Kconfig symbol and want it on, prove it actually
compiled in — do not trust `EXIT=0`:**
1. grep `build/config/sdkconfig.h` for `#define CONFIG_<SYM> 1` (absent = out);
2. `xtensa-esp32s3-elf-nm build/gorb-os.elf | grep <new_fn>` and
   `strings … | grep <new log line>`;
3. sanity-check the `binary size` delta is the right order of magnitude.
Fix a missing symbol by writing the explicit `CONFIG_<SYM>=y` line into
`sdkconfig` (append if absent), then rebuild. Fresh clones get `default y` for
free; it's the *existing* local `sdkconfig` that silently excludes it.

## The config pages are C strings, and a JS syntax error in them is invisible on a radio-less bench

There are two pages, each one giant `static const char[]` of concatenated
`"…"` fragments: `HTML_LANDING` (provisioning at `/`) and `HTML_TZ` (the clock
at `/timezone`). The timezone UI lives only on the clock page — the owner took
it off the network page outright after the first phone tests. The clock page
renders city rows from `/api/v1/config` (`label` + `iana` columns), reads the
phone's zone via `Intl…resolvedOptions()` and auto-applies it; for a zone the
table does not carry it SYNTHESIZES the DST rule client-side from the phone's
own IANA database (bisection of monthly offset windows, refused when the
pattern moves between years — Jerusalem/Casablanca class), and every set is
verified by comparing the phone's offset against the device's measured
`tz_offset` echo. It shows no POSIX rules to a person, ever — the rules are
built in the browser and posted, never displayed. The zone table itself
(`s_tz_tbl`) is machine-generated from the IANA zone of the same name and the
host suite re-proves every row against the machine's tzdata (2025-2028, every
noon + the minute either side of every transition); regenerate rows via
`tools/host_tz_test.py`'s derivation, do NOT hand-edit a rule, and key new
rows on the canonical IANA name (the five `US/*` keys are frozen because NVS
may already store them). `tools/host_tz_test.py` parses and pins both pages
and enforces those boundaries by forbidding tz UI on the landing page and
`posix`/<select> on the clock page. Three ways the pages break in a
way curl + a working server can hide:

1. **Unbalanced parens across fragment boundaries.** Each C line looks fine in
   isolation; only the compiler's concatenation makes `say(a+(b?'x':'')+(c?'y':''))`
   wrong, and a phone renders the result as a page stuck at "loading…" with an
   empty network list AND an empty timezone dropdown — because the first parse
   error kills the entire `<script>`, so `status()`, `scan()` and `tzload()` all
   silently never run. That empty-dropdown symptom (reported from a real phone)
   was exactly this: one extra `)`. Reviewing source line-by-line shows nothing.
2. **A blocking handler starving the page's parallel requests.** See the
   `NET_CMD_SCAN`/`scan_collect` code: the original ran
   `esp_wifi_scan_start(block=true)` on httpd's single worker, so the status poll
   and the tz fetch queued behind a 5-10 s scan. Sequential curl never shows it;
   a browser fires its first requests at once.
3. **A call to a function whose definition was deleted.** Syntax-clean, every
   substring pin green, and the button just does nothing on the phone. This
   happened for real while writing the synthesis engine (a rewrite dropped
   `gmtRule`'s definition but left its call). The host suite lints both pages
   for calls to functions that do not exist — keep that lint green too.

`tools/host_tz_test.py` now rebuilds the page bytes exactly as the compiler does
(concatenate the fragments, C-unescape) and parses the `<script>` with esprima —
a syntax error fails the host suite. This is the file's own rule in action: what
the no-radio bench can't observe must be lifted to a host check. **Keep that check
green whenever you touch the page.** (Verified by mutation: re-injecting the `)`
turns it red.) esprima is not stdlib — a missing parser is a loud SKIP, not a
pass, so `pip install esprima` if it skips. The suite is self-verifying:
`python3 tools/host_tz_test.py --mutations` re-runs it against fourteen
deliberate regressions (deleted synth engine, rubber-stamped echo check,
hand-edited rule, shrunk buffer, …) and every one must fail it with a named
verdict; a battery that cannot fail is not a test.

## Simulating the phone side of provisioning on the radio-less bench

The host has no WiFi radio, so "a phone joins orb-xxxxxx" is the one link the
bench cannot supply — but everything AFTER the first join is testable as a
station: once a phone has provisioned the orb onto a network the bench can
also reach (the home LAN), the orb boots connected, its LAN IP answers curl,
and APSTA lets tooling open the AP (`POST /api/v1/wifi/ap {}` — the config
page has no button for this, see the h_wifi_ap comment) while the station
stays up. That exercises the full page, the connected-ack AP close (watch
serial for `SoftAP down (lease held, hold expired)`), and captive DNS.
Credentials SURVIVE app re-flashes — only `erase_region 0x9000` (nvs) +
`0xD60000` (storage) removes them — so keep one bench orb provisioned and
re-wipe only to test first-boot provisioning itself.
(Older notes mentioned `www/provisioner.html.gz` and `WIFI_SSID` baked from
`.env` at build time: that mechanism is NOT in this tree — provisioning is
entirely `HTML_LANDING` in `orb_net.c`. Verify before repeating that claim.)

## Bench has no WiFi radio — verify what you can, and say what you can't

There is no wireless adapter on the host, so the phone-side captive-portal popup
("Sign in to network") and any real client joining the AP cannot be proven here.
What IS verifiable on the bench, via USB serial + the LAN the orb joins as a
station:
## One clone, one orb — the .orb-target binding (2026-10-11)

Two ESP32-S3 orbs share this bench and enumerate identically (`303a:1001`); only
the MAC in the `/dev/serial/by-id` name tells them apart. Each clone therefore
binds exactly one orb through the **untracked** `.orb-target` at its root
(`ORB_LABEL`, `ORB_BASE_MAC`, `ORB_SERIAL`, `ORB_IP`), and every device tool in
`tools/` refuses to run without it: no bare `/dev/ttyACMx` and no hardcoded orb
IP survives in tracked code. `tools/orb_bind.sh` (shell) and `tools/orb_bind.py`
(python) implement the gates — port must exist, esptool `read_mac` must match
the bound MAC, `ip neigh` must confirm the LAN address — and the per-orb
sidecar flock in `/tmp/orb-<label>-<mac>.lock` makes a second writer fail
loudly. Binding follows the working copy, not the branch: switching branches
never moves a tool onto the other orb. Current bindings: clone `gorb-os-2` →
orb-A (`44:bd:8d:60:80:88`, ttyACM0, `192.168.2.32`); clone `gorb-os` → orb-B
(`44:bd:8d:78:26:18`, ttyACM1, `192.168.2.34`). Both orbs are provisioned and
self-join GTROUTER2 at boot (~2.5 s observed 2026-10-11); neither runs an AP
unless opened via `/api/v1/wifi/ap`. If a DHCP lease moves, boot-capture to
read the new address and fix `ORB_IP` there — the ip-neigh gate re-proves it
every run. If usbipd re-attach renames ACM numbers, nothing breaks: tools ask
by by-id path, not number.

- Serial console: plain opens work (user is in `dialout`; re-attach via usbipd
  can drop it — `sudo` then). Flash/boot capture: `tools/capf.py <secs> <log>
  [--no-reset]`, `tools/cap.py <secs> [reset]`, `tools/grab_boot.py`.
- `/api/v1/status` over LAN (state/fw/slot/img/rssi/synced/ap/assoc_fails).
- AP open/close lifecycle + `captive DNS answering for …` via
  `tools/ap_cp_test.sh` (opens AP while station holds a lease, checks the station
  stays connected+synced, fires the probe endpoints, closes AP, greps serial).
- **Audit loop**: flash, then confirm `/api/v1/status`'s `fw` is the git hash you
  built (`-dirty` = uncommitted edits). Ship = test = commit, same bytes.
  **Caveat, measured 2026-10-11: that check can pass while lying.** `fw` comes from
  `esp_app_desc_t`, which CMake stamps at *configure* time and caches; `idf.py build`
  recompiles changed sources but keeps the old stamp. A tree with uncommitted edits
  therefore flashed an image reporting a clean hash it was never built from (observed:
  `art_bank.c` edited 14:46:10, image built 14:46:31, stamp still plain `54094e8`).
  Trust the stamp only after `idf.py reconfigure && idf.py build`, and rely on
  `tools/serflash.sh`, which now reads the version out of the `.bin` itself
  (`esp_app_desc_t.version`, byte 48), compares it to `git describe --always --dirty`,
  and exits 1 without flashing on a mismatch. The verdict goes to
  `/tmp/serflash-audit-<ORB_LABEL>.log` (per orb, so the two clones' audits
  never overwrite each other); `/tmp/serflash.sh` is a wrapper for it.

Anything needing a radio to *observe* (the popup, real DNS answer to a client)
belongs in `tools/host_tz_test.py` by lifting the pure logic verbatim — see how
`dns_build_reply`, `retry_delay_s`, `sta_leave_is_self_inflicted` and the tz
block are tested. Do not claim a radio-dependent behavior is verified.
## Clock themes from watch-designer kits (tourbillon/animated second hand)

`tools/bake_clock.py` takes a layered watch kit: base gear frames, hands/,
layers/{dial,glass,outer}, an optional tourbillon/, and a manifest.json carrying
every pivot/centre. The only kit the repo still ships is
`assets/sources/av-kit/watch-aviator-kit.zip`, the source of the embedded bank;
it has no tourbillon. Do NOT trust the manifest's tourbillon `centre`: it is the
renderer's own claim and was found wrong by half a texel on both axes (kit2:
declared [233,233], measured centroid (232.5,232.5) — a texel corner no integer
pivot can sit on, giving a 1.70 px hub bob). Run
`python3 tools/kit_check.py <kit.zip>` on the zip BEFORE baking and let it gate
the drop; see `docs/tourbillon-axis-spec.md`. The gate needs a kit WITH a
tourbillon, so it takes an exported kit, not the shipped av-kit. After the
bake, `python3 tools/sim_tourb_bob.py` re-measures on the emitted pair. The three
tools share one implementation (sim_tourb_mark's integer maths, kit_check's red
gate), so they cannot disagree.
The bake itself emits `assets/clock_assets.{bin,h}` as one pair; they are
generated together and must be committed together — the firmware reads
`clk_theme` by layout, so a stale half of the pair is a corrupt dial.
System python has no PIL; use a venv with `pillow numpy` for both tools.

    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/prep_watch_kit.py <kit.zip> /tmp/out
    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/bake_clock.py /tmp/out/theme \
        --bg-frames /tmp/out/frames --bg-fps 15 \
        --tourbillon /tmp/out/tourbillon --tourbillon-fps 15 \
        --force-sweep --tick-rate 15 --dump-dir /tmp/preview

The SHIPPED compiled-in bank is **av-kit** (composer preset `Aviator`, 15 base
frames at 15 fps, no tourbillon), not kit3. Its source kit is committed at
`assets/sources/av-kit/` with its `DESIGN_ID`; read that folder's README before
re-baking. It reproduces exactly on the geometry half and to within 59 bytes
(0.005%, one 1-px antialiasing fringe at 932 B stride) on pixels — measured
2026-10-12 by running the recipe below against the committed zip. Verify rather
than believe: the emitted `.h` differs only in its provenance comment, which
records the input dir path, so compare the `.bin` by md5 and the `.h` by ignoring
line 4. `assets/clock_assets.h:3` names the source theme; if it disagrees with the
kit you just baked, you baked the wrong kit.

    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/prep_watch_kit.py assets/sources/av-kit/watch-aviator-kit.zip /tmp/p1/av-kit
    /tmp/bakevenv/bin/python tools/bake_clock.py /tmp/p1/av-kit/theme \
        --bg-frames /tmp/p1/av-kit/frames --bg-fps 15 --tick-rate 15 --force-sweep

The 59-page **flagship** bench bank (`LAT_BYTES_KIT3 = 3858874` in
`main/art/art_bank.c:642`) was baked from `watch-orbital-kit3.zip`, sha256
c910be26dde63eb4... That zip and its prep output left the repo on 2026-10-12;
`git log --diff-filter=D -- assets/sources/` recovers them. The constant is a
pinned measurement of art no longer in the tree, so re-running that bake
requires the kit back from history first. `headless-orbtheme.test.js`'s
whole-container fixture now bakes the shipped av-kit instead: prep it with
`prep_watch_kit.py`, then bake with `--bg-fps 15 --tick-rate 15 --matte off
--bg-occlude auto`, which mirrors the JS side's manifest-derived cadence.

What kit3 measured, kept as the acceptance record: carriage
bbox=(109,46,249,310) pivot=(233,233) centre=(233,233), i.e. header
pivotx/pivoty = (124,187), 0.0000 px from the measured staff axis. kit2 was the
rejected case (0.707 px off-axis) and is likewise no longer committed.

Layer mapping (kit drawOrder -> compositor slots, verified against the ink radial
surveys — check the same numbers before trusting a new kit's art into a slot):
the gear base is the `--bg-frames` delta bank; the tourbillon is the SECOND-HAND
slot as a frame bank (`--tourbillon`, max 16 frames, per-frame diff rects chained
vs predecessor exactly like the delta bank); the dial is `static1` via
`hands.order=[2,3,0,1]` (over the carriage, under the hands); the glass sheen is
the single `clock_overlay.png`; the bezel is matted INTO the bank frames by
`prep_watch_kit.py` because its rim is fully opaque and no layer above reaches
r>=220 (minute hand tops out at r=199) — a runtime ring would cost PSRAM blends
the canvas restore serves for free. Glass must stay the overlay: the kit draws
hands UNDER glass, so folding it anywhere else is a fidelity loss.

**Bake-time static composite (`--matte`) — the two compositing methods.** A full-coverage
static layer (the dial disc, the glass) otherwise pays a `blend_px` on every commit across
the whole marked span. `tools/bake_clock.py --matte auto` folds such a layer into the
background it sits on (the plate, or every frame of the delta bank) so it costs *nothing*
at runtime, and records it in `clk_theme_t.matte_mask` (bit per hand slot, bit 5 = the
glass); `clock_view.c` then omits the layer from both the LVGL objects and the L4 hook's
job list, and the bake ships that sprite blank. `--matte off` keeps every layer a runtime
composite. Default is `auto`, and auto means PROVED, never assumed: a fold is legal iff
the layer never rotates, blends NORMAL, and its alpha is exactly 0 on every pixel any
LOWER layer can ever paint (a rotating hand can paint its whole ink annulus about its
centre — `_ink_reachable`). Alpha 255 does not license it either: matted, a lower layer
would paint *over* the opaque one instead of under it. Read the printed verdict before
changing the flag — on the orbital kit both statics are refused (dial: 64,151 of 96,406
ink px under the carriage's r=0..221 reach; glass: 112,267 of 113,422 px, max alpha 54),
so this art stays the runtime-composited type and `matte_mask` is 0. `--matte force`
overrides the proof for measurement only and prices the lie in pixels: it does reach
8-9 fps (avg commit 136-144 -> 82-94 ms, `dial 0/0 glass 0/0` in the blend telemetry,
1.24M -> 613k blends/s) but changes 20,109 px of the panel by up to 63 RGB565 steps —
the carriage loses the dial's tint and the hands lose the glass sheen. **A forced fold is
never a shippable setting**; if a theme needs the fps, the art must change so the proof
passes (the way the bezel rim does), not the proof. Fold nothing when `plateFollow` is
set — a baked-in layer would slide across the glass with the parallax. The fold composites
in the device's own RGB565 (`_mix565`), never PIL's 8-bit alpha_composite, so a proved
fold is bit-identical to the blend it replaces — verified by an `auto` bake reproducing
the shipped `clock_assets.bin` byte for byte.

Firmware rules this dial depends on (`main/clock/clock_view.c`):
- `tourbillon_advance()` swaps the shown frame off the wall clock (15 fps) and
  marks ONLY the union of the outgoing+incoming baked diff rects at the current
  angle; the orbit itself is plain hand rotation (manifest's seconds/60x360).
  First draw marks the full swept box. Park/resume resets `s_tourb_shown`.
- The sweeping carriage's orbit step marks the ROTATED INK, not the box:
  `tourbillon_ink_mark()` (from `apply_angles`, i==2 only, AND from
  `tourbillon_advance` for the INCOMING frame at the swap angle — the baked
  diff rects are chained against a neighbour frame, and a 15 fps bank on a
  ~7 fps tick often jumps two, so neither rect covers the g->f delta; the
  incoming chord stamp is what makes the swap complete) rotates every source
  row's ink chord of frame f — per-frame chords `s_tourb_cx0/1` are measured
  once off the alpha plane into PSRAM — into the `s_wp_*` device-row spans,
  then releases <=466 `mark_rows` calls. Both orbit poses are stamped (the
  vacated pixels are the other half of the job). Fallbacks that keep the box
  union: non-L4, `s_shadow[2]`, bank never composited, chord alloc failed.
  TWO stamp invariants, both invisible to the fps counter when broken:
  1. The forward rotation uses `rot_sin_cos()` — paint_layer's exact
     interpolated /1024 trig pair — scaled **\*64** into the `>> 16` products
     (1.0 == 65536). The first builds fed LVGL's /32768 `trigo_sin` table
     (or sinma\*32) straight into the `>> 16`: stamps landed at HALF radius,
     the carriage's outer ring was never restored/repainted, and it ghosted
     a full revolution while a crisp diff-box rectangle glared next to it.
  2. A chord whose rotated segment is WIDER than tall (|dx| > rows) stamps
     its full span on every row it (+rpad) touches — the row-walk, which is
     correct and tight for steep segments, marks only a ~7 px window per row
     there and leaked the counterweight tail near the 12 pose. rpad rows
     beyond a steep segment's ends extrapolate x by step16 (that IS covered).
  Verify any change to stamps or paint_layer with
  `python3 tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`: it ports both integer pipelines against
  the baked alpha and asserts every pixel either pose paints lies inside the
  tick's marked spans (dense at cardinal poses; exit 0 = clean).
- `mark_rows(y0,y1,x0,x1,force)`: rows are handed to the display through
  `display_mark_rows_clocked`; the GIF push marks force=true (its pixels
  reached mirror+canvas without the panel ever seeing them). The commit hook
  `clock_layer_commit` then FLUSH-GATES non-force rows: it snapshots each
  marked row, restores+re-paints, and `display_commit_row(y, memcmp)` sends
  only rows whose rebuild came out different (LVGL-chunk rows always ride,
  via `s_chunk_row`). Without this the disc of a sweep re-DMAed ~200k px of
  identical pixels per tick and capped fps at 5 even after the blends were
  cheap. `s_row_snap` alloc failure = every marked row flushes (safe legacy).
- `mark_all_layers()` early-returns when `clk_theme.tourbillon_n > 0`: with
  full-coverage art (translucent dial + glass over every row) the classic
  per-tick full-box re-mark measured 5 fps; letting only the movers mark their
  own rows (apply_angles / tourbillon_advance) runs 9-10 fps. Classic themes
  (tourbillon_n == 0) keep the old discipline verbatim — do not "optimise" it
  for them without re-reading the freeze-bug note above that function.
- `paint_layer()` has an identity fast path at ang % 360 == 0 (the dial and the
  glass never rotate); `blend_px()` is the single blend implementation both
  loops share — keep it that way.
- `CLK_GIF_THROTTLE 2` does nothing on this art (tick period exceeds the GIF
  deadline, so every tick is due anyway); measured no fps change. Leave at 1.

The kit's orbit SWEEPS: bake with `--force-sweep --tick-rate 15`. tickRate must be
>= the bank fps so `hand_beat_ticks()` yields one tick per beat (tpb<=1 = true
wall-clock sampling every tick); a low tickRate RE-QUANTISES the sweep to that
many beats/s and at rate 1 is indistinguishable from ticking. Sweep costs no
extra fps now (see the rotated-ink + flush-gate rules above): both the orbit
step's marks and the DMA are confined to the carriage's actual ink, so dial
and glass only re-blend where the carriage passed. Quantising to tpb=2
(`--tick-rate 7`) measured NO higher fps (pipeline already saturated) for
its steeper steps — rate 15 is the best sweep setting. Classic tick art
keeps the same discipline either way — sweep lives in the theme, not the code.

Steam-punk is NOT lost: `git checkout assets/clock_assets.bin assets/clock_assets.h
main/CMakeLists.txt --` restores the shipped 1.85 MB pair (it rebuilds identically
from the same --bg-gif orb_bg.gif command, and /tmp/steam-punk-assets-backup holds
a copy). Swap the pair, `idf.py build`, and the full `idf.py flash` (the assets
partition write is the slow ~3 min part — detach, per above).

Verified on the bench at fw with this theme: boot logs `tourbillon: 15 frames @
15 fps on the second-hand slot, orbit about (233,233)`; sweep steady 6-7 fps
(avg commit ~145 ms, px/s ~0.8-1.0 M, no Guru) with `tools/sim_tourb_mark.py`
CLEAN — every pixel either pose paints provably lies inside the tick's marks.
(The earlier "9 fps" figures were taken while the stamps ran at half radius:
unmarked pixels were neither restored nor re-blended, i.e. a third of the
work — and of the artifacts — was simply being skipped. Correct marks cost
what they cost; speed work must shave per-px blend cost, not coverage.)
Before the rotated-ink marks + commit flush gate, sweep ran 5 fps at ~450
rows/commit.
`--dump-dir` writes the bake's own inspection PNGs (canvas_NN, stale_NN must be
black, sprites over checkerboard). They are debug output, not inputs: point it
outside the repo, nothing reads them.

## The theme upload path, and what the no-card bench can prove about it

`main/sd/orb_sd.c` owns the card, the name/path validators, the write-to-temp
rename and the upload destination. `main/art/orb_theme.c` owns the container
header parser and the boot/load decisions. `main/net/orb_net.c`'s `/themes` page
owns the browser side. An SD socket empty and a radio off prove nothing about
that path, so `tools/host_sd_test.py` covers it on the host instead. It needs no
IDF, no radio and no card, and takes ~2 s. It lifts the shipped
functions verbatim from `orb_sd.c` / `orb_theme.c` / `orb_net.c`. Same rule as
`host_tz_test.py`: the real functions, not a re-typed copy. Its checks:

- **Name + path validation**, every rejection reason spelled out (traversal,
  embedded slash, leading dot, empty, over-long, all-dots, control bytes,
  over-ASCII), the built path compared byte for byte, the buffer boundary
  measured both ways (`outsz` that must fit and one byte less, with a canary
  tail that catches any write past it).
- **The geometry reader's refusals**, the shipped parser must reject each
  damaged-blob case with the *exact* reason the firmware emits. Non-NULL is not
  enough: the first draft of this suite let the tourbillon-extent mutation
  survive because a different downstream check caught the same blob for a
  different reason, and the vacated check stayed invisible.
- **The header reader**, real containers from `tools/orbtheme.py` plus
  deliberately damaged ones (bad magic, wrong format/version, wrong device,
  wrong panel size, implausible blob, oversized payload, torn header, trailing
  words). One known-answer row: a header copied out of the orbital-arrow-3 bake
  is embedded in the harness and must be ACCEPTED. A stub of `esp_rom_crc32_le`
  sits in the harness and must
  match the ROM's init/final inversion, that row is what proves the stub.
- **`/themes`' own `<script>`**, parsed with esprima (a missing parser is a
  loud SKIP, never a pass), every function it calls matched against the
  functions actually lifted out of the shipped `.c`, and each critical
  interaction pinned to a substring: the delete confirmation, the upload's
  `XMLHttpRequest` with `upload.onprogress` (a `fetch` cannot report upload
  progress), the 50 KB chunk, the `.part` cleanup in the `finally`.

- **Two source lints over the card code**, `chunk_lint()` and `lock_lint()`. See the
  next section: neither is a lift, both are regex over `_code_only()` output.

`python3 tools/host_sd_test.py --mutations` re-runs the whole suite against
24 deliberate regressions (validator loosened, each extent check deleted, the
header's checksum ignored, the delete confirmation gone, the destination builder
trusting its caller) and every one must fail it with a named verdict. A battery
that cannot fail is not a test.

One check is a lint rather than a lift: `err_t_lint()` reads the return types out of
`orb_sd.h` / `orb_theme.h` and fails any call site in `orb_theme.c` / `orb_net.c`
that puts `!` in front of a function declared `esp_err_t`. ESP_OK is 0, so
`if (!orb_sd_ensure_mounted())` means "if the card is mounted, refuse". It compiles
with no warning, and a bench with an empty socket cannot observe it. That inversion
shipped here first and is the mutation this lint owns.

Four real defects this suite caught before any card went in: a stray `'` in a
JS comment that broke the whole `/themes` `<script>`, `theme_file()` building its
own path with `snprintf` and validating nothing (it now delegates to
`orb_sd_theme_path()` so the extension append shares the validator and the
bounds check), a signed/unsigned comparison in the GIF-frame check
(`n_first < t->gif_frames + 1`, which silently promoted a `uint16_t` to a signed
int), and `orb_theme_upload_begin()` testing `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` with `!`,
which inverted the mount test and refused every card that was present. glibc's
`strlcpy` differs from the IDF one, so the lifted code is renamed
`host_strlcpy`. That rename is the only edit the lift is allowed to make.

What hardware proved, and the two rules it cost a boot cycle each to learn, are in
the next section. Numbers measured on the board are there too.

## The card lock: two rules, and why the bench cannot see either

`orb_sd_lock()` is a plain non-recursive FreeRTOS mutex, and `esp_http_server`
services every session from ONE task at priority 4. A second take by the holder never
returns. The symptom is not a slow route: it is every web route stopping at once while
the dial keeps drawing and ping keeps replying, because the render task and lwip do not
need that mutex. A card lock bug therefore looks like a dead web server.

Three defects of that family were found on the board, none of them observable on the
bench:

1. `s_chunk` NULL on the upload path. The upload opens its `.part` with a bare `open()`
   and never passes through `card_open()`, so nothing had reserved the 4 KB buffer.
   FatFs stored through NULL and the dial died with a StoreProhibited at the end of a
   successful 3.87 MB upload. `ensure_chunk()` now sits at the top of
   `verify_container()`, `copy_into()` and `orb_theme_upload_begin()`: in
   `orb_theme_upload_begin()` it is an early no before the radio carries anything, and
   in the other two it is defensive and idempotent. `chunk_lint()` owns that rule.
2. Self-deadlock in `orb_theme_scan()`. It holds the lock across the whole directory
   walk and validated each entry through `card_open()`, which took the same mutex. An
   empty themes directory runs that loop body zero times, so the bench passed while the
   device wedged on the first real theme. `card_open()` is now a wrapper around
   `card_open_locked()`, and the scan calls the `_locked` pair.
3. The reverse mistake, made while fixing 2. `card_open_locked()` kept a
   `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` call, which takes the mutex its caller already holds. The
   device's own new log line named it on the first boot: `waiting for the card: held by
   "httpd" for 16002 ms`.

Rule 1: nothing reachable between a take and the give that matches it may take the
mutex again. Call the `_locked` variant.

Rule 2: a lock holder must already be mounted. Mounting is the one card operation that
cannot happen while the mutex is held, because `orb_sd_ensure_mounted()` takes it.
`orb_theme_boot()` runs from `main.c:72` and `orb_sd_init()` only from `main.c:138`, so
the boot install can mount the card only through `card_open()`'s wrapper. With the check
in the core instead, a staged theme burned attempt 1 of 3 over a file the card had the
whole time, and the dial drew the baked-in bank with nothing to blame.

`lock_lint()` checks both rules: the held region is the first take to the LAST give
(not a depth walk, because `orb_theme_scan()` unlocks on an early return and a depth
walk then stops before the walk body), followed through the local call graph, plus the
cross-file lockers in `orb_sd.c`, plus a mount call before the first take. Both rules
are pinned by named mutations: `locked core re-asks for the mount it is holding` and
`card_open stops mounting before it locks`. `_code_only()` is not cosmetic: with doc
comments left in, `lock_lint()` reported three deadlocks that did not exist, from the
function names the comments discuss.

### Measured on the board (2026-10-10, SD16G SDHC 29.4 GB @ 20 MHz SPI)

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Upload 3 867 066 B | `state:stored` in 38.8 s and 48.1 s, RSSI -53 to -74 |
| `cfg.recv_wait_timeout` | 30 s. At the IDF default of 5 s a live 3.87 MB stream was aborted |
| Scan with one real theme | `listing /sd/themes` / `checking "kit3"` / `1 entry listed in 6 ms` |
| Scan with the directory emptied | `0 entries listed in 3 ms` |
| Load (full CRC of the container) | `stage "kit3": container verified in 2952 ms`, routes still answering |
| Boot install | `install of "kit3" from the card: ok`, `theme "kit3" from slot-B`, then `slot-A` |
| Clock | `art bank mapped: 3858874 B ... from slot-B`, bank at `@0x830000` not `@0x420000` |
| Delete the installed theme | `deleted "kit3" from the card (still installed on the dial)`, free_mb +3 |
| Delete, then reboot | boots straight into slot-B with no install attempt and no burned retry |
| Pending theme with the file gone | `theme install attempt 1 of 3 failed`, falls back to the baked-in bank |

Two method rules, both learned the hard way in this session:

- **Read the address from the serial log, not from memory.** The device prints
  `esp_netif_handlers: sta ip:` when DHCP completes. Two full rounds were lost probing a
  stale `192.168.68.251` after the board joined a different SSID at `192.168.2.32`. Every
  symptom of that (TCP connects, zero bytes, whole server "wedged") looked like the mutex
  bug and was not.
- **Capture serial unfiltered, then grep the file.** A keep-regex in the first capture
  script matched `orb_theme|orb_sd|load|card` and dropped the watchdog and boot lines, so
  a hang looked like silence. `orb_sd_lock()` now logs the holder task name, reports the
  wait every 5 s, and logs an ESP_LOGE on a same-task reentry; that instrumentation named
  the deadlock in one boot, which is the argument for putting it in before the bisecting.

## The on-dial menu, and the one knob gesture you cannot inject from the bench

`main/menu/menu_view.c` adds a third screen. `main/input/input_router.c` is still the
only reader of the knob; it now has two levels. Roaming, a turn swaps
MENU → HUE → CLOCK and a press arms the screen you are on. Armed, the screen owns the
turn and a long-press returns to the clock. Long-press no longer cycles screens —
one button cannot mean "next screen" two ways depending on how long it is held.

**The card calls must not run where the widgets are touched.** `orb_theme_scan()`
takes the card mutex for its whole walk and `orb_theme_stage_load()` measured 2952 ms;
either can additionally queue behind a browser upload holding that mutex for 38–48 s.
The router's poll is an `lv_timer` on the LVGL task and is simultaneously the dial's
animation and the only input reader, so both calls live on the `orb_menu` worker task
(4096 B, priority 3, matching the httpd worker that already runs them). `host_menu_test.py`
lints this: a blocking call in any function that also calls `lv_label_set_text()`/
`lv_obj_set_style_*()`/`lv_obj_invalidate()` fails the suite, and
`the card scan moves back onto the LVGL task` is one of its named mutations.

### Verifying a screen nobody turned a knob on

The encoder is three GPIOs driven only by a finger, so before this the menu and the
picker had never been entered on hardware. `GET /api/v1/screen` is the bench door:

| Query | Effect |
|---|---|
| `?to=<slug>` | switch to a registered app (posted, applied on the LVGL task). Slugs come from the table in `main/app/orb_app.c`: `clock`, `color_picker` (alias `hue`), `menu`. An unknown slug is a 400 whose body lists the known ones — the value itself never reaches the response. |
| `?turn=N` | N synthetic detents, **electrical** sense: +1 = CW = up/right on screen |
| `?push=1` | one synthetic tap |
| `?hold=1` | the long-press escape gesture, which goes to the entry flagged `is_home` — the dial (C8) |

**Read the cursor before you trust a turn.** Until 2026-10-11 the menu said nothing about
where its highlight was — `repaint()` moved the highlight and that was the only answer — so
three scripted walks drove it blind and every one of them believed it had arrived somewhere
else. Two pressed the `IT_AP` stub three times thinking they were choosing DISPLAY; a third
fired `JOB_TZ` twice and left the dial on Europe/London while believing it had committed the
dim timeout. `menu_view_apply_input()` now logs `cursor <PAGE> row N/M: <label>` on every
move, and a walk that ignores that line is not to be trusted. `page_hint()`/`page_title()`
already carried the page name; the log was the half that was missing.

The clamp at row 0 is the specific thing the echo distinguishes: `if (next == s_cur) return;`
means a turn already at the top of the list produces **no event at all**, which from the LAN
is indistinguishable from a detent the decoder swallowed — the exact symptom the owner
reports as "sometimes the notch does nothing". Note also that the injected sign is the
electrical one: `?turn=-1` walks the cursor DOWN from row 0, because the delta arrives at the
router already signed and does not pass through the encoder's own sign handling.

Inject the long press too: it is the only exit from an armed screen, so a broken
one soft-locks the dial with rotate belonging to the list. Prove it — verified here
as armed zone-list → `menu idle` → `screen -> clock` inside one 10 ms poll.

Two bench traps, both hit for real on this feature:

- **Reset before a scripted walk.** A previous run can leave `s_inj_delta` set if it
  exited before the 10 ms poll drained it, and the next run then swerves to another
  screen ~500 ms in with no request behind it. Real detents cannot go stale, so this
  is harness hygiene, not a firmware defect.
- **Wait for the radio, do not sleep.** After a reset the association ladder costs
  ~5 s at −57 dBm; poll `/api/v1/status` until it answers or the first request of the
  walk times out and reads as a crash.
- **A stalled HTTP body at rssi near −55 is the bench RF, not the firmware.** The
  symptom: status line and headers arrive, body bytes never do; small bodies
  (<1 KB) still complete. Hit for real on 2026-10-12 with the /apps listing: the
  same hammer run failed `/themes` on a known-good image as badly as on the new
  one (ok=2/20 control vs ok=0/20 suspect, rssi −55 both). Bisect the antenna
  before bisecting the commit: run the identical walk against a previous image
  and compare rates.

It reaches only `input_router_request()`/`input_router_inject()`, cannot touch the
card, radio or bootloader, and sits beside `/api/v1/reboot` and `/api/v1/ota`, which
are far more capable. `input_router.c` is still the only code that calls
`lv_screen_load()`.

### The trap that cost two wasted hardware runs

**One detent per event is a clamp, not a bug.** `menu_view_apply_input()` moves the
cursor a single row whatever the magnitude of `delta`, so a burst of `+2` in one poll
is ONE row of travel — deliberate, because a fast turn must not fly past the row you
were heading for. Both my first drivers asked for `turn=2` expecting two rows, landed
on TZ instead of THEME, and I read that as a routing bug twice. Drive it with one
`?turn=1` request per row, and read the row you are on from the server's own
`/api/v1/sd` `themes` list rather than from an assumed order.

Also: the card must actually hold a *non-active* theme. Staging the theme already on
the dial is correctly refused with "is already on the dial" and leaves `pending` empty,
which looks identical to a broken staging path. Upload a second container under a
different name to test the real transition.

`/api/v1/status` does **not** report a staged theme. Read `pending` from `/api/v1/sd`.

## How the knob is measured, and what decides how it feels

`main/knob/knob.c`. `knob_rot_isr` shares one `s_prevAB` across A and B, decodes each
edge through `kQuadTable`, and calls `knob_edge()` — the one function that decides
whether an edge was a click. `knob_edge()`, `knob_detent_from_net()` and
`knob_detents_rounded()` are pure over plain integers and take time as an argument, so
`tools/host_knob_test.py` lifts them verbatim and replays synthetic AB state sequences.
That is the only way any of this was getable right: the three reported failures below
each survived being read twice.

**A detent is an arrival at the valley, not a count of edges.** The valley is the
quadrature state the detent spring parks the shaft in — measured on this unit as
`knob: valley 3 (11)` at boot, i.e. A=B=HIGH. On arrival at it, the net travel since
the last arrival decides the direction, and one arrival credits at most ONE detent.
`knob_poll()` then settles anything left over once the stream has been idle for
`KNOB_SETTLE_MS`, which recovers clicks whose valley edge never arrived.

Why the edge count had to go. The reference driver (and my first two attempts here)
committed after a full detent of net raw travel, and the owner's report after that
design was three complaints in one sentence — **"it moves mostly one but sometimes two
and sometimes none"**:

| Report | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| first notch does nothing | the click's fourth edge lands past the click stop, so a hand stopping on the notch never reaches a whole-detent gate | net travel, not edge count; the settle recovers the short click |
| sometimes two | make-bounce lands extra net steps in ONE notch, and an edge counter cannot tell that from two clicks | one credit per valley arrival, plus `KNOB_CLICK_GUARD_MS` (20 ms — above any contact bounce, below any wrist) |
| sometimes none | edges lost between pin and ISR leave no valley arrival | the idle settle rounds the stranded net into detents |

Rocking a knob is still inert, which is the hysteresis the reference needed and the
reason a naive commit-on-every-edge is not the answer: out-and-back nets zero, so
nothing is credited. `host_knob_test.py` asserts all three failure modes and catches
16/16 mutations, including "restore the edge-count rule", so the regression cannot come
back quietly.

**`KNOB_STEPS_PER_DETENT` is a property of the unit, not a feel knob.** Read
`knob.spd10` on `/api/v1/status` after turning it by hand: 40 means four edges per
click as assumed, 20 means the constant is wrong by half — indistinguishable from the
hand. `knob.vhits` against `knob.shits` splits the detents by how they arrived; a
session that is mostly `shits` means edges are being lost between the pin and the ISR,
which is wiring or interrupt latency and no retuning of the rule will fix.

### The turn direction, and where it is allowed to be interpreted

The encoder sits on the case side above the dial's centre, so the arc the finger
travels is not the arc the list scrolls. `KNOB_DIR_DOWN_IS_CW` (0 now) is the single
switch. What a committed turn *means* comes only from `knob_row_step()` and
`knob_col_step()` in `knob.c`; `knob_take_delta()` keeps the raw electrical sense
(CW = positive) so the bench door and any future gesture code see the truth.
**A screen that negates a delta itself is a bug** — `host_knob_test.py` fails on
`(delta > 0 ? 1 : -1)` appearing in a screen file.

The owner's ruling, on record because the mounting fixes it and the code cannot
derive it:

> the direction that means **DOWN** is the good direction for **LEFT**;
> the direction that means **UP** is the good direction for **RIGHT**.

`knob_col_step()` is `knob_row_step()` negated, so a future horizontal screen calls it
and cannot drift out of that agreement, and flipping the one constant moves both axes
together. Today: CW = up = right, CCW = down = left. The hue editor deliberately reads
the raw delta instead — a value is not a list position — with the reason recorded at
its call site.

Verified on hardware 2026-10-10 (`/tmp/dirwalk2.py`): baseline `US/Mountain`, one
injected `?turn=-1` then a press applied `US/Pacific` (one row down) and one
`?turn=+1` applied `US/Central` (one row up).

**Bench trap, hit for real:** `?turn=N` is the *electrical* sense and the screens now
map it, so any saved walk script that used `?turn=1` to step **down** a list now steps
**up** and silently selects the wrong row — `/tmp/homewalk.py` still passes its
escape-hatch checks while pressing `AP (stub)` instead of `TZ`. Read the row you reached
out of the serial log (`menu: AP selected`), not out of the script's intent.

**Injection proves nothing about the encoder.** `/api/v1/screen?turn=N` enters at
`input_router_inject()` and never touches `knob_rot_isr`, so `knob.raw`/`knob.det` stay
at zero through a fully successful injected walk. Any claim about quadrature, the
valley, the commit rule or debounce needs a finger on the knob and a read of
`/api/v1/status`'s `knob` block (`tools/turnsession.py` drives exactly that).

### Measured on the board (2026-10-10, menu build)

| Step | Result |
|---|---|
| Boot | `menu ready (worker up, 62 zones, no card)`, `router running (poll 10 ms, 3 screens)`, boots on the clock, no new crash |
| Theme scan off-task | `listing /sd/themes` → `1 entry listed in 5 ms`, dial kept 6 fps throughout |
| Worker stack | `job N done, 608 B stack never used` (scan) / `336 B` — headroom, not exhaustion |
| TZ apply through the menu | `US/Central` → `US/Eastern`, status offset `-300` → `-240`, then restored to `US/Central` via the same path |
| Theme stage through the menu | `stage "kit4": container verified in 3900 ms` → `staged "kit4"` → `job 1 done`, poll still answering |
| Decline the staged theme | `orb_theme_unstage()` from the menu's own row; `pending` back to `""` |
| HSB two-level gate | unarmed turn logged `screen -> clock` and no colour change; armed turns logged `-> H = 211/212/213`, push logged `push H -> S` |
| Cost | app 0x15e590 → 0x15ff30 (~7 kB) |

Two refusals that are features, not bugs — both cost me a debugging cycle here:

- Staging the theme already on the dial answers `"x" is already on the dial` and leaves
  `pending` empty, which looks exactly like a broken staging path. Test with a second,
  non-active container.
- `orb_theme_stage_load()` rejects a container whose header slug disagrees with its
  filename: `the file calls itself "kit3" — rename one of them`. Copying a `.orbtheme`
  to a second name therefore does not create a second theme; patch `W_SLUG`/`W_TITLE`
  and re-run `_hdr_crc()`, leaving the pixel payload byte-identical.

`tools/host_menu_test.py` covers what the bench cannot reach by hand: the cursor clamp
at both ends, the no-wrap rule, the 62-row windowing, the back row on both sub-pages,
the damaged-theme row that must be *shown* with its reason, and a worker result
landing while the screen is disarmed (which must not paint a list on the title card).
`--mutations` must stay red on all twelve; one round of it "survived" eleven because
the lifts were taken at import and the mutated text never reached the compiler — if
you touch this suite, check it can still fail.

  the deadlock in one boot, which is the argument for putting it in before the bisecting.

## Testing the Watch Composer: deploy it to Windows and drive Chrome there

The composer (`tools/watch-composer/Watch Composer.dc.html`) is a browser app. Its real
behaviour lives in canvas pixels, file dialogs, and a localStorage asset store. This WSL
box has no Chrome, so those three can only be exercised on the Windows side. Ask for
"test the composer" and the expected action is this one command:

```sh
cd /home/greg/docker/Projects/gorb-os
python3 tools/watch-composer/deploy_test_folder.py            # -> /mnt/c/Users/greg/Downloads/test
/tmp/nodeenv/bin/node tools/watch-composer/_selftest/headless-test.js   # the WSL-side gate
```

The deploy script copies the four files the app loads (`Watch Composer.dc.html`,
`support.js`, `dial-renderer.js`, `watch-engine.js`) into the target root, and all of
`_selftest/` into a `_selftest/` subfolder. That depth is load-bearing: the selftest
pages load `../watch-engine.js`. Flatten the layout and every probe 404s its engine.
Each copy is compared by sha256 before and after, because a drvfs cache can hand Chrome
stale JS with no error at all.

Then open the two URLs the script prints:

- `file:///C:/Users/greg/Downloads/test/Watch%20Composer.dc.html` — the app itself
- `file:///C:/Users/greg/Downloads/test/_selftest/export-test.html` — the end-to-end suite

Both need internet. `support.js` boots React, ReactDOM and Babel from unpkg, and the
`<helmet>` pulls ~40 families from Google Fonts. Offline, the composer renders blank and
looks broken when it is not.

Verdict rules, so a skipped block cannot read as success:

- `export-test.html`: the tab title must read `PASS` and the last report line must be
  `RESULT: PASS`. The custom-art block prints its own line starting `custom art: backdrop
  in the plate pixels`. Missing that line means the block never ran. The five evidence
  lines, and `?ONLY=feat` to run the pixel block alone when the suite stops printing
  without a verdict, are listed by `deploy_test_folder.py`.
- Headless suites: each must print a nonzero check count. A suite reporting green with a
  suspiciously low count stopped exercising something.
- After editing the component class, regenerate `_selftest/component.js` (the recipe is in
  `_selftest/README.md`). It is an extracted copy, and a stale one keeps the UI suites green
  while they test last week's code.

What only a human can confirm in the app, after uploading art in the Dial and Hands tabs:

- the plate backdrop paints under an openwork dial and the apertures, and clips to the dial
  circle under a drawn case — that is "Background art · behind the geartrain". Under Case
  "None" the same art runs to the panel corners instead, because its own pixels are the case
- the dial-face art reads as a printed dial: chapter print, numerals and applied markers stay
  legible ON TOP of it, and openwork and the apertures cut it away — "Dial surface art"
- the two slots are independent: different images, different offsets, and one image may fill
  both without the store counting it twice
- the fit gate refuses an upscale, and names both sizes in the refusal
- Zoom rescales the art about its centre without moving it off its offset, and the alignment
  guide's frame tracks it exactly — the box drawn on the stage is the box that was painted
- clicking the dial or a hand selects it AND opens the pane that can change it; clicking the
  same pixel again steps down through whatever overlaps there, so nothing under the top layer
  is unreachable
- and the pane then SCROLLS to that item's own row (a hand's pivot fields, a complication's
  row, the art section of the slot being panned) — switching tabs without moving the view is
  the half-fix that reads as working until the field you wanted is below the fold
- Escape, or the Deselect button under the stage, clears the selection outright: with the dial
  selected every pixel of the face is the dial, so no click can deselect it; the Guide button
  beside it pins the overlay on or off independently of the selection
- dragging the dial pans the art slot the chip names, not the other one
- dragging a hand moves its arbour, and while the drag is held the hand stops sweeping so the
  geometry under the cursor cannot rotate away
- the virtual hand reads: dashed sprite, arbour cross, sweep ring and the intended-direction
  ray. Set Facing to bring the ink onto the ray — art drawn pointing anywhere but 12 is fine
  as long as Facing says where it points
- a hand-sprite pivot outside the visible ink is accepted and reported as such, and the
  thumbnail frames the ink inside the grown plane
- "None" (no case) draws NOTHING at all: no metal, no rim shade, no mask, and no clip on the
  author's own art, which is what carries the device's case and must reach the panel corners.
  The composited pixels are identical with that layer shown or hidden, and identical at any
  opening — the slider only says where the hardware begins, so the guide can draw the ring
- the alignment guide's reference (hub cross, 24 spokes from it, range rings, comp
  centres) is pinned to the watch and does not move while the dial art is dragged; the
  art's own frame and centre are drawn in blue precisely so the two are not confused
- a window set to a round aperture is visibly round, including its frame and its digit
- the hand-sprite pivot marker drags, and hub-snap lands on the visible ink centroid
- a transparent sub-dial drops its plate, keeps its hosted window on the bare dial, and
  its pointer keeps a real pivot instead of a hub cap
- Export ships the layer kit plus the `.wckit`, and reloading keeps every upload

The asset store is one shared `file://` origin in Chrome. Every local copy of the
composer — repo folder, Downloads copy — reads and writes the same `wc.assets` key, so
clearing it in one clears it everywhere. That sharing is also what makes redeploying safe:
uploaded art survives the copy.

